t there's you know a  lot of things you have to do to be  careful if you were actually going to  use this so so this is just kind of an  illustration okay now 4000 variables are  far too many for me to visualize and to  plot and to understand kind of the  relationships of these variables and how  they drive these outcomes and so with  the principal component analysis or the  SVD are going to allow us to do is  essentially learn kind of eigen genetic  sequences so if I SVD this big X matrix  then I would get a 216 by 216 u matrix a  diagonal Sigma matrix and V would have  216 by by 4,000 4,000 columns and each  row of V would be essentially an eigen  gene sequence it's kind of the gene  sequence that captures most of the  variation among these 216 patients the  second one would be the second most  variation eigen gene sequence and what's  neat is that instead of visualizing all  4,000 dimensions of these patients  genetic sequence what I can do is I can  plot just the first few eigen gene  sequences for each patient and so  instead of this 4000 dimensional space I  can project down into a low dimensional  space in terms of principal component  one principal component two and  principal component three and if I'm  lucky then the patients that do and  don't have cancer might cluster in this  space and that would give me information  about how I could then understand what's  different about them what's the same how  to maybe predict cancer in a new patient  something like that okay so let's run  this example we're gonna load the  ovarian cancer data set we also are  going to learn the groups so in addition  to this big matrix of observations  in this OBS matrix we also have a vector  of labels  that's 216 tall and it basically says  they have cancer or they don't have  cancer so the first half is yes the  second half is now okay and we're going  to take this X matrix this OB s V does  this observation matrix and we're going  to compute its SVD just like before and  we're gonna plot the cancer and  non-cancer patients in those first three  principal components okay so so that's  what we're gonna do before we do that  scatter plot let's just look at the  actual singular values of this data set  so these are the the log of the diagonal  elements of Sigma and this is the  cumulative sum to show how much variance  is captured by the first are our  singular vectors and this is exactly  what I want to see when I compute the  SVD of a data matrix notice that there  is this massive energetic group of only  a few singular values that capture a  huge portion of the energy or variance  of the data set so you have this large  portion of structure and then you have a  relatively fast roll-off where there's  very little information down here so  capturing keeping only the first three  or five or ten of these singular values  and singular vectors you're going to  capture a large portion of the variance  between all of these patients genetic  information and you can see here even if  you only capture the first singular  vector you'd have about 55 percent of  the of the variance of this data matrix  so that's that's really promising so now  what we're gonna do is we're going to  take our observation matrix this this x  matrix and we're gonna project it into  the first three principal components so  basically I'm going to take these first  three rows of V transpose kind of V 1 V  2 V 3 so this is a 3 by 4,000 and for  every patient I'm just going to take  their genetic sequence and take the dot  product with these 3 rows and I'm going  to get 3 numbers out and those 3 numbers  are what I'm going to plot and these  principal component one two and three  and I'm gonna plot different symbols for  the patients with cancer and without  cancer based on their group and here's  what we have in the plot so you can see  that the X's are the patient with cancer  the circles are the patients without  cancer and very nicely this data  actually does kind of separate in these  principal component directions so you  get reasonably good separation in these  first three principal components and if  I included let's say you know the first  five or ten or twenty I probably would  get even better separation of those two  categories but this provides a low  dimensional human interpretable way of  understanding this very high dimensional  genetic information because I'm  projecting into this low dimensional  kind of eigen genetic space where I can  plot how these distributions fall out  okay so principal components is super  useful for visualizing high dimensional  data that has some statistical  distribution and using it to understand  kind of the structure of that low  dimensional the low dimensional  structure about data okay thank you

